dex/skills/stdo-work/references/recovery.md\",\n  255|       \"root\": \"product\",\n  256|       \"selector\": null,\n  257|       \"sha256\": \"d7f7f118eea492bb64456a56c4cb4987e6e23acd8b3c0143ba87cf5ec9a92c80\"\n  258|     },\n  259|     {\n  260|       \"engine\": \"workspace-regular-file\",\n  261|       \"object\": null,\n  262|       \"path\": \"build_tenants/codex/skills/stdo-work/references/release-install.md\",\n  263|       \"root\": \"product\",\n  264|       \"selector\": null,\n  265|       \"sha256\": \"ec7cb5a407f6690936d0bf79ab5fc8796c2cfca4c34772f61ceff4b68f337cfb\"\n  266|     },\n  267|     {\n  268|       \"engine\": \"workspace-regular-file\",\n  269|       \"object\": null,\n  270|       \"path\": \"build_tenants/codex/skills/stdo-work/references/tools.md\",\n  271|       \"root\": \"product\",\n  272|       \"selector\": null,\n  273|       \"sha256\": \"8bb49c0dfe0eb87fbfbe5063c4a43e15f074e25fdd4ff90ffdec24ef14e75c8b\"\n  274|     },\n  275|     {\n  276|       \"engine\": \"workspace-regular-file\",\n  277|       \"object\": null,\n  278|       \"path\": \"build_tenants/codex/skills/stdo-work/source-routes.json\",\n  279|       \"root\": \"product\",\n  280|       \"selector\": null,\n  281|       \"sha256\": \"09972e4af512645c43207aa3edd5114e1e81c24ef344c76a6af2e99852920403\"\n  282|     },\n  283|     {\n  284|       \"engine\": \"workspace-regular-file\",\n  285|       \"object\": null,\n  286|       \"path\": \"build_tenants/codex/tests/verify_package.py\",\n  287|       \"root\": \"product\",\n  288|       \"selector\": null,\n  289|       \"sha256\": \"65ec9851967a60ab6f25f4c988b2ddbc05642ce44ae71401d6f1bf99f5ec0086\"\n  290|     }\n  291|   ],\n  292|   \"id\": \"urn:stdo-skills:T001:claude:cross-family-review:1\"\n  293| }\n  294| \n\n######## [01] review:USER-OUTCOME.md sel=None ########\n    1| # Original user outcome selected for this review\n    2| \n    3| The user's actual instructions in this session included:\n    4| \n    5| > the tools have not been maintained and i'd apply stdo design principles itself the tools need to harmonise with stdo 3.0.1\n    6| \n    7| > we are going to create two build tenants for skills codex & claude\n    8| \n    9| > the requirements are the same but the build tenants are different especially as each may need tuning to its type\n   10| \n   11| > yes the are decoupled, this is a test, eating our own dog food,\n   12| > 1. pin stdo 3.0.1 as the static released methodology against specification_method\n   13| > 2. as executive build out of stdo skills, use the current tooling to build the skills\n   14| \n   15| Root selected a separate root-level stdo_skills Product, with shared WHAT and independent host-specific HOW tenants, under the verified immutable released STDO3.0.1 basis. This statement records the user's outcome and Root's scope selection; it does not import the candidate's wording as its own oracle. No RC formalisation, remote publication, general benefit, reliability or complete methodological qualification is claimed.\n   16| \n\n######## [02] method:standards/EVALUATION.md sel={'lines': [177, 248]} ########\n  177| ## 3. Independent assessment\n  178| \n  179| A constructor's self-review is construction output, not assessment. Where\n  180| promotion needs independent assessment, the assessor evaluates the exact\n  181| live subject, authority paths, proof and installed state, not the\n  182| constructor's summary (P6, and P0 with its premise that a step's output is\n  183| a candidate until evaluated; instance premise (c) of §1 Foundation to law).\n  184| This section decides what counts as independent assessment. Which\n  185| boundaries require it is a policy choice of the Spec Method, owned by\n  186| [TICKETS §7 Execution and verification authority separation](TICKETS.md#execution-and-verification-authority-separation-up-007);\n  187| a governing basis may add boundaries.\n  188| \n  189| Protocol: where method qualification applies\n  190| ([§7 Method self-qualification](#method-self-qualification)), the subject is\n  191| frozen by identity before material reference exposure; elsewhere it is\n  192| stabilised by an immutable identity or an equivalent exact-subject relation\n  193| sufficient for the claimed assessment. The assessor records its\n  194| expectations from the governing source before opening the candidate; it\n  195| reads the subject at that identity or relation, never a mutable working\n  196| tree; it makes no repair; it does not assess its\n  197| own authorship. Reusing the constructor's judgment does not satisfy\n  198| independence; shared mechanical evidence may be reused, judgment may not\n  199| (ACTIVATION §7). Binding a returned assessment to its producer, subject,\n  200| basis and evidence authenticates the result; it establishes neither the\n  201| assertion provenance of its expected answer nor the execution provenance\n  202| of its run, which\n  203| [FOUNDATIONS §4 Parallel derivation and mutual assurance](FOUNDATIONS.md#parallel-derivation-and-mutual-assurance)\n  204| requires separately (P6, P3). Membership in a plan authenticates nothing,\n  205| an unauthenticated result satisfies no required assessment, and a result\n  206| bound to another subject satisfies no planned assessment\n  207| ([FOUNDATIONS §4 Conjunction of results](FOUNDATIONS.md#conjunction-of-results));\n  208| no cryptographic or transport protocol is prescribed for the binding (P6). Any post-exposure revision is a declared intervention: it\n  209| is either a method constituent with its own qualification boundary or a new\n  210| qualification subject, and it cannot retroactively make the original\n  211| frozen construction pass. This law prescribes no actor type or count,\n  212| review-round count, engine, prompt or orchestration (P6).\n  213| \n  214| Author identity is the party responsible for the content of a realised\n  215| subject. It is distinct from actor identity, whoever performs one\n  216| operation, which is an identity and not an authority\n  217| ([FOUNDATIONS §2 Authority kinds](FOUNDATIONS.md#authority-kinds)).\n  218| Authorship is one of the conditions on\n  219| which the independence of an assessment rests; the evidence conditions are\n  220| the other (P6, P3).\n  221| \n  222| Reporting a defect, supplying a counterexample or identifying behaviour\n  223| already required by governing law does not by itself author the realised\n  224| subject. Contributing wording, design or policy that is adopted into the\n  225| subject, including through directions for a particular repair, is authorship\n  226| of that contribution. A\n  227| reporter may assess a later repair only when the existing assessment grant,\n  228| authorship relation and evidence-independence conditions permit it; a\n  229| reporter gains no review authority from having found the defect. A person\n  230| who authored an adopted repair cannot supply its required independent\n  231| assessment by calling that authorship a finding (P6, P3).\n  232| \n  233| Independence rests on authorship and on the evidence conditions above, not\n  234| on a new context. The assessor may read the same governing sources as the\n  235| constructor. A fresh context alone establishes no independence, and it\n  236| supplies no review authority the author lacks: an author that opens a\n  237| fresh context to review its own candidate is still its author, and its\n  238| review is self-review, which stays construction output and cannot be\n  239| relabelled as independent assessment (P6;\n  240| [TICKETS UP-007](TICKETS.md#execution-and-verification-authority-separation-up-007)). A contribution received from another author stays that\n  241| author's wherever it is carried, and that author's reading of it supplies\n  242| no independent acceptance of it (P6).\n  243| \n  244| Independence across actor families is declared, not assumed. Whether two\n  245| activations of one model family are independent for a semantic judgment is a\n  246| policy the governing basis states for the claim; the default where it is not\n  247| stated is that they are not independent.\n  248| \n  249| \n\n######## [03] method:standards/EXECUTIVE_BASELINE.md sel={'lines': [957, 1009]} ########\n  957| ## 10. Derived Reviewer frame\n  958| \n  959| (P6, P5)\n  960| \n  961| | Frame element | Reviewer |\n  962| |---|---|\n  963| | evaluation family | Is this the exact claimed subject and basis? Does it satisfy the applicable method and project relations without competing authority, proof masking or an unevaluated composition boundary? For each finding, which evidence-bound severity and technical implications follow? |\n  964| | subject and basis | exact candidate, claim, governing method and project authority, scope, exclusions, evidence population, Product-selected severity scale, review carrier and declared independence conditions |\n  965| | material manifold | claimed relations; causal producers and consumers; authority seams; competing paths; failure, recovery, installed and reconstruction surfaces where claimed; test and fixture semantics; historical counterexamples; a finding's cause, blast radius, workaround, repair complexity, regression risk and uncertainty where supportable |\n  966| | coordinates | exact candidate, source basis, authority revision, configuration, dependency, evidence population, severity scale, installed subject, review time and Reviewer identity |\n  967| | governing invariants | RF-001 to RF-017; the execution relation (ACTIVATION §1 and §3); independence and traceability (EVALUATION §3); assurance boundary and qualification freeze (EVALUATION §1, §3 and §7); applicable Product, design, identity and release law |\n  968| | authority | bounded evaluation only; review access yields no construction, transformation, admission, publication, mutation, decision, disposition or next-activation authority |\n  969| | evidence | independently acquired live authority, candidate implementation, tests, artifacts, history, operational evidence, mutations, replay or reconstruction, and reproduced observations material to the claim and the technical triage |\n  970| | capability envelope | declared method, domain, implementation, assurance, technical-triage, tool, access, context, counterexample-search and independence competence |\n  971| | exclusions | editing the candidate, implementing a repair, assigning priority, disposing the release or MVP boundary, selecting the next action, substituting the Worker's summary, assurance-owned semantic reconstruction, flattening unbound evidence, silent scope expansion |\n  972| | result | one core result with findings, counterexamples, evidence-bound technical triage and residuals sufficient for the Executive to assign priority and apply the existing disposition where the basis is decidable |\n  973| | invalidation | a material change of candidate, authority, basis, evidence population, severity scale, dependency, configuration, scope or independence |\n  974| \n  975| When independent review is required, and what makes a claimed review\n  976| traceable, are\n  977| [TICKETS UP-007](TICKETS.md#execution-and-verification-authority-separation-up-007),\n  978| with the further boundaries a governing basis adds; what counts as\n  979| independent is [EVALUATION §3](EVALUATION.md#3-independent-assessment);\n  980| EVALUATION §1, §3 and §7 decide the exact assurance boundary a","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"persistedOutputPath":"/Users/jim/.claude/projects/-Users-jim-src-apps-specification-methodology-skills/628dfe7c-13d9-4c76-8146-64fd9787ee38/tool-results/barha86i9.txt","persistedOutputSize":684221},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01CppEL3JFKQ7Jdn3jzRX6oW","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
